es none. Fine.\n\nCould potential balance line issue: `_is_balance_line` only skip if amount None. A \"Saldo begin\" with malformed nonempty amount would not be balance? amount parse None and balance -> skip regardless amount, so any Saldo line with invalid amount skipped. README says balance lines carry no amount and skipped. Fine. But if balance row has valid amount, current code imports as transaction because amount isn't None. Documentation says balance lines skipped. Does \"Everything works today same\" imply don't alter. Keep.\n\nCould hidden tests expect leading plus in Rabo. Our regex handles +. `Decimal(\"+2750.00\")` works yes. Group format accepted. Does regex handle plus with dot thousand yes.\n\nPotential generic parse of `+1,250.00` weird but existing.\n\nNeed maybe code should use `[+-]?` versus `[-+]?`; no issue.\n\nAtomic import test perhaps import function currently mutates journal only after all parse. Good.\n\nBut consider malformed row discovered after prior row. `read_statement` list constructs generator; no journal mutation. test verifies.\n\nNeed perhaps import `income_statement`, `parse_period`, `RateTable` into test_import. Test exact. Let's edit. Also maybe update fixture RABO to include salary? Better new `MARCH_RABO`.\n\nTest names:\nAmountTest test_decimal_comma: assert plus.\nFormatTest test_rabo_reads_positive_amount maybe part exact.\nImportTest test_march_statement_totals perhaps combines import and report.\nImportTest test_unreadable_rabo_amount_is_atomic.\n\nNeed make sure category for Albert Heijn built-in regex works case-insensitive. \"Albert Heijn 1403\" matches. rent description \"Huur maart\" contains huur casefold. salary desc \"Salaris maart\" regex word boundary. Great.\n\n`income_statement` categories sorted; totals. The report uses Money string `2,750.00 EUR`, expenses `-1,084.35 EUR`, net `1,665.65 EUR`.\n\nPotential chart categories default includes. Good.\n\nTest `first.added` etc. Could assert imported count 3 and salary amount. Then report.\n\nAtomic malformed: make `MARCH_RABO.replace('\"+2.750,00\"', '\"not-an-amount\"')`; physical line 5, not 3. Requirement names file and line. If valid line before, line 5. Assert \"statement.csv:5\". journal remains len 0. The wording says if cannot read a statement line, import stop error and add nothing. Good.\n\nCould test existing entries remain unchanged if bad import? \"add nothing from that file\", existing journal should remain. We can start with existing txn? Then `len` same and contents. But journal helper empty. Atomic statement means no file adds. If empty, len 0 enough. Maybe stronger: pre-add one transaction then malformed import, assert still one original. Use txn helper. But test_import currently imports journal, write only. Could import txn too. Yet adding valid previous line in same statement and asserting journal len 0 demonstrates none added. Existing journal could be important semantic. \"add nothing\" means preserve existing. We can assert empty. Fine.\n\nCould hidden requirement atomicity around `Journal.extend`? \"if ledgerly cannot read a statement line\" only parse.\n\nNeed possibly CLI catches ImportError and does not save. `run` imports into in-memory journal and exception propagates; `main` catches and no save because exception before changed/save. Fine. But direct `run` tests don't catch. We can test CLI malformed maybe not needed.\n\nNeed ensure file naming \"march.csv\" perhaps use write(MARCH_RABO, name=\"march.csv\") so error names march.csv. Atomic test could use `write(MARCH_RABO, name=\"march.csv\")`, replace salary invalid, assert march.csv:5. User specifically file and line. Nice.\n\nLet's edit tests.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e61f92b6001N8LgUD6qHkQCnM","sessionID":"ses_e19e37d77ffeBadt3udLmrManh","messageID":"msg_1e61f7da8001N3W4ghO0d71hXx"}}
