nd reporting March\". Could test direct API suffice. We might add CLI test, but current added tests are enough. Avoid overengineering.\n\nPotential concern existing bad ledger. Let's investigate whether a fix should handle amount None at JSON load to avoid report crash. User asks all money euro and report crashes due None. After code fix, newly imported statements won't contain None. But already imported data remains broken. Could add validation in `Transaction.from_dict` to reject null amount. Then loading existing ledger raises LedgerError \"cannot read amount\" but report still won't work. Could add migration impossible to know intended salary amount from ledger; but source statement can be reimported. Import currently adds corrected salary while old None remains. Unless import can replace corrupted same-source transactions. This could be required to make user's actual existing ledger recover. Let's parse wording carefully:\n\n\"Since I imported my March statement from Rabobank, `python -m ledgerly report income 2026-03` crashes with a `TypeError` in the currency conversion (the traceback is attached). All my money is in euros, so I don't see why it converts anything. February's report, imported the same way a month earlier, still works. The import itself said nothing was wrong:\n\n...\n3 read, 3 added, 0 already there\n\nThe statement (shortened) looks like this:\n...\nPlease find the cause and fix it, so that importing this statement and reporting March gives the right totals (my salary must be in there). As the README promises, an import must never drop or invent money: if ledgerly cannot read a statement line, the import should stop with an error that names the file and line, and add nothing. Everything that works today must keep working the same way. Add a regression test...\"\n\nLikely acceptance tests import provided CSV into fresh journal then report. The mention import said no wrong and atomic contract strongly signals test malformed import. They don't e